WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.440
I’ve co-founded two YC companies and learned a&nbsp;
lot in the process, so here are my key takeaways&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.440 --> 00:00:10.880
from Y Combinator. Start Small: It can be easy&nbsp;
to focus on big companies that you might be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.880 --> 00:00:16.080
competing with in the first days of starting a&nbsp;
company, but that’s a mistake. Big things have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:20.800
small beginnings. Every company starts as&nbsp;
nothing more than an idea, but what makes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.800 --> 00:00:27.760
companies get really big is consistent compounding&nbsp;
growth over a long period of time. Growth rate is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.200
by far the most important factor in a startup's&nbsp;
success. It’s what every venture capitalist values&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.200 --> 00:00:38.320
most when considering an investment so you need to&nbsp;
start thinking about growth immediately. Focusing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:38.320 --> 00:00:42.960
on growth rates instead of absolute numbers&nbsp;
can be extremely motivating. This is because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.960 --> 00:00:47.600
the smaller you are, the easier it is to&nbsp;
have a high growth rate. If you only have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:52.320
a few hundred users, doubling that number in a&nbsp;
month is completely doable, and that will start&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:56.960
compounding pretty quickly. As you get bigger,&nbsp;
doubling every month might not be possible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:56.960 --> 00:01:02.880
but 20% monthly growth is usually sustainable for&nbsp;
companies with strong product market fit, and 20%&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:08.800
monthly growth works out to a ten x increase over&nbsp;
a year. I’ve personally seen this with my YouTube&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:12.880
channel. The number of subscribers is still&nbsp;
really small, but I’ve been growing the channel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.880 --> 00:01:18.400
20% per month on a very consistent basis, which&nbsp;
is extremely promising. So if you’re watching this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:23.360
and you’re not subscribed, please do me a favor&nbsp;
and help me keep that growth going. Y Combinator&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:28.480
is just 3 months long and many enter the program&nbsp;
with just an idea or basic prototype. Without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.480 --> 00:01:33.120
fail, the companies that get the most attention&nbsp;
at demo day have the highest growth rates. It’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:38.400
way better to be a company that went from making&nbsp;
$1,000 at the start of the program to $10,000 in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.400 --> 00:01:43.280
just 3 months, than be a company making a million&nbsp;
dollars without any growth. If you’re ever feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.280 --> 00:01:48.640
like you’re in a David vs Goliath situation,&nbsp;
going up against massive competitors, just compare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:54.240
your growth rates. The biggest companies in the&nbsp;
world aren’t growing 10x in a year, but you can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.720
if you build a great product that people&nbsp;
love. And that brings me to lesson number 2:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.720 --> 00:02:03.920
get into build mode. No job or college degree&nbsp;
can fully prepare you for being the founder of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.920 --> 00:02:09.520
a startup, they are all too specialized. Building&nbsp;
a company from scratch requires bringing together&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:14.560
so many different skills and this is one of&nbsp;
the hardest, but also most rewarding aspects&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.560 --> 00:02:19.040
of entrepreneurship. Paul Graham says that “if you&nbsp;
want to start a startup, it’s good to know about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.040 --> 00:02:24.880
multiple, unrelated things. Startups often&nbsp;
depend on 4 or 5 different kinds of knowledge,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:30.000
and it’s great if they can be all found in 2&nbsp;
founders” - it’s rare to start your company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.000
already knowing about all the relevant&nbsp;
disciplines required to succeed though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:39.200
so that’s why it’s so important to get into build&nbsp;
mode. When I say “build mode” I mean being willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:43.840
to roll up your sleeves and learn the relevant&nbsp;
skills to accomplish the task in front of you.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.840 --> 00:02:48.080
You might be a great programmer, but no one will&nbsp;
sign up for the app you built if you can’t figure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:52.960
out how to promote it. So you might need to learn&nbsp;
how to write compelling blog posts or run digital&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.960 --> 00:02:57.520
ads in order to start growing. When I started my&nbsp;
first company, I wasn’t a very good programmer. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:02.480
was able to throw together basic prototypes, but&nbsp;
I didn’t have much experience actually growing a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:07.040
digital product. So I spent months coding and&nbsp;
eventually got to a point where I was pretty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:12.160
comfortable writing Python. I’m still not an&nbsp;
expert, but I know enough to be dangerous and that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.160 --> 00:03:16.960
helps me manage engineers effectively. My second&nbsp;
time through Y Combinator was a little different.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.960 --> 00:03:22.800
A few months before I started YC, I was managing a&nbsp;
large team at my previous company and had lots of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:28.880
support. My day to day was mostly management and&nbsp;
I really wasn’t in that “builder” mindset anymore.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.920
The first company had scaled to over 50 employees&nbsp;
and it didn’t make sense for me to spend time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:38.640
writing code when I had a whole team of talented&nbsp;
developers who were way better than me. As soon as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.640 --> 00:03:44.000
YC started, I really focused on shifting back into&nbsp;
build mode. Since I was comfortable programming,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:48.560
I spent time learning more about marketing and&nbsp;
branding. I figured out how to produce video&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:52.960
content, which is really valuable when you’re&nbsp;
selling products online, and also led to this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:57.280
YouTube channel. You shouldn’t be afraid to learn&nbsp;
new things when you’re working on a startup.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.280 --> 00:04:01.600
You’re not going to be an expert, but learning&nbsp;
about a particular discipline will help immensely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:07.440
when you eventually start hiring. All of this&nbsp;
will be a lot easier if you follow lesson 3: avoid&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.440 --> 00:04:12.480
distractions. Starting a company is a full-time&nbsp;
endeavor. In some cases, you can get started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.480 --> 00:04:17.280
while still working a 9 to 5 or finishing college,&nbsp;
but once you start taking your startup seriously,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.280 --> 00:04:22.480
you’ll quickly realize the value of focus. When I&nbsp;
went through Y Combinator, moving to the bay area&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.480 --> 00:04:27.360
was a requirement, and one that I really benefited&nbsp;
from. I was completely broke, which meant living&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.360 --> 00:04:33.200
in a fun part of San Francisco was off the table.&nbsp;
I wound up moving to a run-down house in Sunnyvale&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.200 --> 00:04:38.320
which isn’t exactly a nightlife hub. That was a&nbsp;
huge benefit though. With no distractions, I was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.320 --> 00:04:43.200
able to focus entirely on learning new things,&nbsp;
writing code, and testing startup ideas. I had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:43.200 --> 00:04:48.880
tried to work from other places but never had much&nbsp;
success. There are always fun events happening on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:53.600
college campuses and working from a friend's house&nbsp;
wasn’t productive for me either. There is a lot of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:58.560
discussion about burnout in the tech world, but&nbsp;
I don’t think that really applies to the early&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.560 --> 00:05:03.440
days of a startup. If you’re working on a big idea&nbsp;
that you’re really passionate about, you shouldn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:08.400
burn out. When I started my first company, I spent&nbsp;
plenty of late nights programming, but it didn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:13.280
feel like work to me. I really enjoyed the process&nbsp;
of learning and building new things I thought of.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.280 --> 00:05:17.760
Now, this is all a lot harder if you’re&nbsp;
completely alone, which is why co-founders&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:17.760 --> 00:05:22.960
are so important. And lesson 4 is to really focus&nbsp;
on building relationships with your co-founders.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.960 --> 00:05:27.520
The experience of building a startup is very&nbsp;
unique, and it can be hard to relate to if you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.520 --> 00:05:32.240
haven’t done it before. This is why entrepreneurs&nbsp;
love to work with venture capitalists who have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.240 --> 00:05:36.400
actually started companies before. And having&nbsp;
a team around you that feels the same way about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.480
avoiding distractions and just building is so&nbsp;
important. But you don’t need to be spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.480 --> 00:05:44.640
every waking hour working in front of a computer.&nbsp;
Getting to know your co-founders on a deeper level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:49.280
is one of the most critical things you can do.&nbsp;
Ideally, you’ll already have experience working&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:53.040
with your co-founders when you start your company,&nbsp;
but it doesn’t matter if you’ve known each other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.040 --> 00:05:58.240
for years, or just a few months, you can always&nbsp;
build a stronger relationship. Understanding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:58.240 --> 00:06:03.360
what really motivates a person is vital to any&nbsp;
good working relationship, and motivation is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.360 --> 00:06:08.960
complex and emergent property. Oftentimes, what&nbsp;
drives a person to build a startup could trace&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.960 --> 00:06:14.080
back to their childhood or upbringing, and you&nbsp;
usually don’t get to know someone on that level&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:19.120
around the watercooler. Not every conversation has&nbsp;
to be a psychoanalysis, but building trust between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.120 --> 00:06:24.560
the co-founding team will pay dividends for years&nbsp;
if it’s done right. Plus, one of the amazing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.560 --> 00:06:29.120
things about bringing talented people together&nbsp;
is that their experiences can cross-pollinate one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.120 --> 00:06:35.120
another. In that Sunnyvale house we had people who&nbsp;
had studied economics, biology, computer science,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.120 --> 00:06:39.760
and electrical engineering. We had a great time&nbsp;
just hanging out and talking about what we had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.760 --> 00:06:44.880
each learned in college and trying to summarize&nbsp;
complex topics succinctly. Everyone has different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.880 --> 00:06:49.680
life experiences and sometimes great things&nbsp;
can come out of combining disciplines. Taking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:49.680 --> 00:06:54.720
advantage of these unique situations requires&nbsp;
being ready to iterate, which is lesson 5. We&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.720 --> 00:07:00.160
tried dozens of startup ideas in that hacker house&nbsp;
and every time we iterated on our ideas we got a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.160 --> 00:07:04.080
little bit better. It’s always tricky to know&nbsp;
when to stay the course versus when to change&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.080 --> 00:07:09.440
directions, but iteration doesn’t always have to&nbsp;
be a binary choice. You might have a crystal clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:14.720
vision of what your product should look like, but&nbsp;
if users don’t love it and you’re not growing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.720 --> 00:07:19.920
something needs to change. You can’t be afraid&nbsp;
to iterate and course correct as needed. In 2012,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:23.440
nothing was working for us. We spent&nbsp;
months building our first product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.440 --> 00:07:28.320
only to completely flop when we launched. So we&nbsp;
increased our pace of iteration. The next product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.320 --> 00:07:34.480
we launched in just 3 weeks. It failed again, so&nbsp;
we decided to try and test a new idea every week.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.480 --> 00:07:39.040
By speeding up the pace of our iterations we&nbsp;
figured out what was actually required to get&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.040 --> 00:07:44.640
feedback on new ideas. Ask yourself: do you need&nbsp;
to build the entire product before launching?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.640 --> 00:07:49.360
Or would a landing page do the trick? Sometimes&nbsp;
all you need is a blog post describing what you’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:54.000
planning to build. If there is real demand for&nbsp;
your solution, people will comment on your post&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.000 --> 00:08:01.200
and encourage you to move forward. It’s useful&nbsp;
to set clear deadlines. Y Combinator is itself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.200 --> 00:08:06.480
essentially one big deadline: demo day. You need&nbsp;
to get up on stage and present your company to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.480 --> 00:08:11.920
hundreds of investors. Clear communication is a&nbsp;
must, so you have to iron out exactly what the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.920 --> 00:08:16.560
problem is and how you’ll solve it. You’re also&nbsp;
expected to show some metrics and they better be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.560 --> 00:08:21.040
up and to the right. YC has weekly dinners and&nbsp;
meetings with partners, which should force you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.040 --> 00:08:25.440
to start thinking about achievable milestones.&nbsp;
You never want to show up with zero progress&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.440 --> 00:08:29.840
because you got distracted. Even if your latest&nbsp;
iteration takes you in a different direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.840 --> 00:08:34.400
that could still be solid progress if user&nbsp;
satisfaction has increased. One of the biggest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:39.360
benefits of Y Combinator is the competition.&nbsp;
All the companies are on a level playing field:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.360 --> 00:08:43.760
3 months to deliver the most progress possible.&nbsp;
You should definitely have longer term goals in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:48.800
mind, but thinking in weekly sprints can really&nbsp;
help keep you focused on what’s most important.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:48.800 --> 00:08:53.680
And at the early stage, there are really only&nbsp;
two things that are important: your product&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.680 --> 00:08:59.040
and your users. Every week you want to be making&nbsp;
your product better in some tangible way and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.040 --> 00:09:04.080
then increasing the number of users getting real&nbsp;
value from your product. That “real value” part is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.080 --> 00:09:08.160
important. There are plenty of ways to game the&nbsp;
stats and make it look like you’re growing when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.160 --> 00:09:12.960
you’re really not. It’s vastly more important&nbsp;
to get a few dedicated users who really love&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.960 --> 00:09:17.840
what you’re building as opposed to adding more and&nbsp;
more users who are indifferent about your product.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:17.840 --> 00:09:22.640
There are tons of activities that feel like work,&nbsp;
but actually do nothing to advance your company&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:22.640 --> 00:09:28.000
at the early stage. When you’re just a team of&nbsp;
co-founders and an idea, you really need to resist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.000 --> 00:09:34.160
the urge to professionalize. This can be really&nbsp;
tempting, because there is immense social pressure&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.160 --> 00:09:40.080
to fulfill a set of criteria for what “work” looks&nbsp;
like. Things like an office, business cards, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:40.080 --> 00:09:45.840
fancy equipment will all make your company look&nbsp;
more professional, but might do nothing to improve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.240
your product. If all of your friends work at big&nbsp;
companies, they might think you’re a complete joke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.240 --> 00:09:54.880
for spending all day coding in a run-down hacker&nbsp;
house. Although that’s changed a bit over the last&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:59.920
year. Getting an office or business cards may make&nbsp;
you feel like you’re more of a “real” company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:05.760
but for many startups, the customer just doesn’t&nbsp;
care about the company behind the product,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:12.080
they just want it to work. Pretty much every YC&nbsp;
company is a mess behind the scenes. No one has it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.080 --> 00:10:17.280
all figured out from day one, so don’t stress over&nbsp;
this. Every successful company goes through rough&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.280 --> 00:10:22.720
patches and has to work through difficult issues.&nbsp;
Entrepreneurship is never easy. But spending time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.720
and money trying to make yourself look more&nbsp;
professional than you are is often a waste of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.720 --> 00:10:31.760
time. To be fair, certain businesses might need&nbsp;
office space earlier than others, or business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.760 --> 00:10:36.800
cards to sell into enterprises at conferences,&nbsp;
but you should really think about whether what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.800 --> 00:10:41.120
you’re doing will have an immediate impact on&nbsp;
your startup. And that brings me to lesson 8:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:41.120 --> 00:10:45.840
you need to learn how to synthesize advice&nbsp;
from different sources. This one is a bit meta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.840 --> 00:10:50.320
but you have to develop a skill for assessing&nbsp;
what’s actually relevant to your startup. It’s not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:50.320 --> 00:10:55.840
uncommon to hear an amazing story from a unicorn&nbsp;
founder during YC and think that you need to apply&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.840 --> 00:11:01.040
their strategy to your business, but this can be&nbsp;
a mistake. YC has lots of incredible partners,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.040 --> 00:11:05.840
each with their own unique startup experiences.&nbsp;
These experiences obviously affect the advice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:05.840 --> 00:11:10.480
that they give and you need to figure out how to&nbsp;
apply new ideas to your business specifically. No&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:15.200
one will ever know your startup as well as you do,&nbsp;
so you shouldn’t be afraid to discount a certain&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.200 --> 00:11:21.120
lesson about startups if you think it just doesn’t&nbsp;
apply. Now, startup advice comes in many forms.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:21.120 --> 00:11:26.400
Some is extremely high level and battle tested&nbsp;
throughout decades. This is advice like “build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:26.400 --> 00:11:31.680
something people want” or “pursue a big market”.&nbsp;
Those recommendations are pretty hard to argue&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:36.080
with, since they are so useful. It would be&nbsp;
very odd to try and build a startup based on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:41.520
the idea of “building something people don’t&nbsp;
want” - but hey, nothing’s impossible. Where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:41.520 --> 00:11:46.160
you want to be more discerning is when it comes&nbsp;
to tactical startup advice, like go-to-market&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:46.160 --> 00:11:51.680
strategy. The best way to launch a new startup&nbsp;
is constantly evolving, so recommendations like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:51.680 --> 00:11:57.040
“create a high-production value video” don’t last&nbsp;
too long. As soon as a specific growth strategy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.040 --> 00:12:02.480
becomes widely known, it usually stops working.&nbsp;
Once every startup is making launch videos,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:07.520
they lose their effectiveness. YC can be a bit&nbsp;
like drinking from a firehose. You’re getting a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:11.920
constant torrent of feedback about what you’re&nbsp;
building, and that can be intense at times. If&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:11.920 --> 00:12:17.440
you’re not doing well, you’ll know it. That’s why&nbsp;
this last lesson is so important: Enjoy Yourself&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:17.440 --> 00:12:22.640
Startups are a long journey. Dustin Moskovitz,&nbsp;
who co-founded Facebook, was once asked what it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:22.640 --> 00:12:27.920
was like to be part of an “overnight success” like&nbsp;
Facebook. I still love his answer, he said: "If by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:34.320
'overnight success' you mean staying up and coding&nbsp;
all night, every night for six years straight,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:39.040
then it felt quite tiring and stressful."&nbsp;
No matter how fast your company is growing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.040 --> 00:12:44.080
there will always be a ton of work. In&nbsp;
fact, oftentimes, the faster you grow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.080 --> 00:12:47.680
the more work you’ll have to do. There are&nbsp;
always growing pains to work through and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.680 --> 00:12:52.000
you’ll be constantly inundated with challenges.&nbsp;
That’s why it’s so important to try and find&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:52.000 --> 00:12:56.400
a problem that you really love working on.&nbsp;
And I’m not just talking about the mission.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:56.400 --> 00:13:01.200
My first company was in the education technology&nbsp;
space, and I thought the idea of helping people&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.200 --> 00:13:07.040
learn would be extremely energizing. The goal was&nbsp;
exciting, but the day-to-day of trying to sell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:07.040 --> 00:13:12.240
products to schools was completely unsatisfying&nbsp;
to me. In order to be able to stay motivated for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.240 --> 00:13:17.600
years, you need to love the mission, the industry,&nbsp;
and the day-to-day. This means you should never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:17.600 --> 00:13:22.000
chase trends. Even if you see a certain&nbsp;
type of company getting funded every month,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:22.000 --> 00:13:26.320
you shouldn’t even think about starting a&nbsp;
company in that industry unless you’re really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:31.920
passionate about the space and could see yourself&nbsp;
really thriving in that environment for years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:35.520
If you want to chat about YC, just leave&nbsp;
me a comment below or DM me on Twitter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:45.440
and please check out this recommended video, the&nbsp;
YouTube algorithm thinks you’ll really like it.

